{
  "query": "A travel booking startup runs a multi-region ticketing platform on Cloud Spanner. After importing organizer and schedule records from a recent acquisition, you observe sustained write hotspots in several tables. You want to apply Google-recommended schema design techniques so that inserts and updates remain evenly distributed and performance does not degrade. What should you implement?",
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Use a timestamp column as the first component of the primary key",
      "explanation": "This creates monotonically increasing keys that cluster new rows at the end of the keyspace.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Use bit-reversed sequence values as the leading primary key column",
      "explanation": "This spreads sequential numbers across the keyspace so inserts touch different splits.",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Heavily denormalize the schema to reduce joins",
      "explanation": "This can simplify reads and reduce join operations but it does not alter the distribution of primary key writes.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Use a composite primary key that starts with a low-cardinality attribute such as region",
      "explanation": "This places many rows under a small set of prefixes which can channel writes to the same splits.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Use a composite primary key that begins with a high-cardinality attribute such as a hashed customer or order ID",
      "explanation": "This increases the number of distinct prefixes so writes are spread across more splits.",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    }
  ],
  "answer": "<p>The correct options are <b>Use bit-reversed sequence values as the leading primary key column</b> and <b>Use a composite primary key that begins with a high-cardinality attribute such as a hashed customer or order ID</b>.</p><p><b>Use bit-reversed sequence values as the leading primary key column</b> spreads inserts across the keyspace so new rows do not cluster at the end of the key range. Spanner sequences that are <b>bit-reversed</b> keep approximate ordering while evenly distributing writes, which prevents leader concentration and reduces contention.</p><p><b>Use a composite primary key that begins with a high-cardinality attribute such as a hashed customer or order ID</b> ensures there are many distinct key prefixes so writes land on different splits and leaders. A <b>high-cardinality leading key</b> like a hashed ID balances load and keeps inserts and updates evenly distributed even during large imports.</p><p><i>Use a timestamp column as the first component of the primary key</i> is incorrect because timestamps are monotonically increasing and cluster new inserts at the end of the key range. This pattern creates write hotspots and degrades performance.</p><p><i>Heavily denormalize the schema to reduce joins</i> is incorrect because denormalization does not address key ordering or leader distribution. It can increase write amplification and does not fix hotspotting that arises from poorly distributed primary keys.</p><p><i>Use a composite primary key that starts with a low-cardinality attribute such as region</i> is incorrect because a small set of leading key values concentrates writes on a few ranges. This increases contention and defeats even distribution.</p>",
